+Admission fixtures must include at least two lawfully retained real exact-version npm artifact/envelope pairs per supported format/provider, with independently checked subject digest, signer/issuer, source/workflow and builder expectations. Include known-answer upstream verification fixtures under their own notices; exercise them through the application wrapper too. Negatives: one-byte artifact/payload/signature mutation; valid signature for another digest/package/version; unexpected signer/issuer/OID; wrong repository/commit/subtree/workflow/builder/build type/parameters; publish-only; absent assertions; unknown predicate/bundle/log version; expired/untrusted roots, malformed certificate, missing/forged timestamp/inclusion/checkpoint and duplicate logs; contradictory multiple attestations; size/depth/time/cancellation and rejected URL/redirect cases. A valid-signature but disallowed identity must fail policy even when crypto succeeds. Keep one unchanged positive beside each mutation to distinguish a rejecting adapter from a working verifier. No signing service, new publication, package hook or rebuild is needed; fixture builders only produce inert local data and do not supply production trust roots. The retained 76-check isolated prototype covers a meaningful subset, not the complete admission matrix. Neither library/root/policy is admitted or positively recommended for production now.
